 "claim": "Crossing knots separated by one port are 2-sums, for any near side. Glue two knot spaces along a simple 3-edge cut with ports X and remove any near line. Let K be a crossing knot with near part U and far part V, and let one of its excluded patterns (u, v) be separated by a single port y: every port pattern compatible with u has y = b, and every one compatible with v has y = not b. Then y lies outside K, U + y and V + y are circuits, and K is their elimination through y. If y is tied to a point of K, that point is alone on its side, the circuit on that side is the tie, and K is tie-equivalent to the other circuit, which is one-sided. Otherwise both circuits are one-sided knots, and the elimination is a class-level step of the closure. Either way K is generated, never new. The proof uses only the join lemma, complement symmetry and minimality. Checked on splices of six census near sides (four plane, two non-planar) and two 10-point plane near sides into four far sides, at every near removal: 33,556 separated crossing knots, all as stated.",
